WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.160
Hey! Welcome to a new Project Kamp update!

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.997
It's early in the morning, because we're going to go to a cork factory.

00:00:06.997 --> 00:00:08.360
Where they're going to harvest the cork.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.800
In a farm and in a factory, 
where they’re going to produce it into useful things.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.840
So, we're going to see how the process works.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.440
It should be exciting!

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.080
So, yeah, like Dave said before, 
it's six in the morning.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.000
So, the streets are still empty.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.000
The landscape is beautiful, covered in thick fog.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.880
The early sun just started to reach out,
with its first golden sunbeams.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.520
We are making our way in Dave’s shakey van on the highway.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.440
A 2,5 hour drive is awaiting us.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.840
First stop is the cork factory called Sofalca.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.480
Located somewhere in the countryside,
between Project Kamp and Lisbon.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.440
There, we're going to meet Charles.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.440
He runs a company called Corklink, 
who is the local supplier for a variety of cork products.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.520
He was kind enough to invite us for a tour,
around the cork tree farms and the factory.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.480
We met him at the parking place of the factory.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.200
We jumped in his car, because it's a bit faster

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.200
And, we continued further south to the cork tree fields.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.720
So, let's see what is waiting there for us!

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.034
It looks like you're in Africa!

00:02:04.587 --> 00:02:05.471
- Safari time!

00:02:08.164 --> 00:02:10.043
There are the lions, giraffes...

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.280
- So, Dave, where are we now?

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.480
So, we're currently at a cork field with a lot of cork trees.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.080
We're going to see how they're going 
to get harvested, how it works...

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.120
To see what a day of a cork harvester looks like.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.800
- Why is it shaking so much?

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:59.040
We're in the back of a pickup truck,
because they're a little hard to access.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.400
All right, so, we're now at the cork harvest place!

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.599
Here, we have Charles!

00:03:32.599 --> 00:03:36.160
He's going to give us a little bit of a tour,
about how the harvesting process works.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.920
- Well...

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.200
We're in Southern Portugal here.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.520
Where most of the cork trees grow in Portugal.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:51.440
During the summer months, all of the cork forests in Portugal
have their barks stripped.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:04:01.107
The guys you can see working here travel around Central
and Southern Portugal, taking the bark off the trees in teams.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.760
So, you can see that the technique they use
is they have an extremely sharp ax.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.760
And, they bash at the tree to just cut the bark.

00:04:09.929 --> 00:04:15.360
They have to hit the bark with just the right amount of strength,
so that the bark gets cracked open.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:21.280
But, they don't damage the membrane 
that's inside the bark covering the tree.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.000
Because, if you damage that membrane,
then the tree can die.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.000
So, these guys, it looks like
they're just smacking away at the tree.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.335
But, actually, it's a finely judged amount of force 
that they're using to hit the trees.

00:04:32.335 --> 00:04:34.320
So, that the bark will fall off naturally.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:42.719
And, funny enough, each of these guys' ax is their own personal ax, 
and they treat it like their pride and joy.

00:04:42.719 --> 00:04:46.000
It's like a concert violinist with their violin.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.040
They won't let anyone else touch their ax,
and they sharpen it all day long.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.116
It has just the right weight for them.

00:04:51.116 --> 00:04:55.849
They know exactly how hard to whack the tree,
when they're taking the cork off.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.360
The cork can only be removed every nine years.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:06.640
Because, otherwise, you don't have enough thickness in the cork, 
to be able to use it to make wine corks.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.520
The joy of cork is that taking this bark off the tree,
it does no damage at all to it.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:13.903
The cork will growback.

00:05:13.903 --> 00:05:18.160
So, nine years later, you can harvest the cork again.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.880
In fact, some of these trees around here are 200 years old!

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.480
They've been harvested every nine years, for 200 years!

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.720
Nothing happens quickly in cork forests.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:13.743
So, a young tree like this one here,
which is maybe four or five years old...

00:06:13.743 --> 00:06:20.520
It's not ready to give up quality cork,
until it's 15 or 20 years old.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:26.520
So, the first seven or eight years,
the cork tree's just left to grow.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.520
And then, afterwards, you can see this tree here...

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.920
This has been harvested once, so it's had its first harvest 
and this is the regrowth.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.700
You can see that the original cork is here, which hasn't been harvested at all.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.358
So, this is the regrowth here.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:45.840
The first harvest of the cork is called virgin cork,
and it's extremely resinous.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.760
You can't use it to make wine corks.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:51.760
So, that's generally used to make expanded cork.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:58.120
The second harvest is generally a reasonable quality cork, 
but it's not the best quality...

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:01.200
The best quality comes in the third, fourth, or fifth harvest.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.972
And, some of these trees around here are 200 years old.

00:07:04.972 --> 00:07:11.360
They can give some fabulous quality cork,
 which is used for natural wine corks of the best quality.

00:07:13.018 --> 00:07:19.680
These amazing cork forests in Portugal 
are actually also a fabulous carbon sink.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:23.640
Because, these trees, they'll grow for 200 years or more.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:30.280
Whilst with other wood products, you have to cut the tree down, 
and that ends its purpose of absorbing carbon.

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:34.280
These cork trees carry on growing forever,
so you're just removing the bark.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.880
And, meanwhile, the tree every year for its entire lifespan...

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:41.680
It's sucking carbon dioxide out of the atmosphere.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:49.960
And, all that's left over is the cork bark,
which is processed into cork to make a biodegradable product.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:56.960
So, it's really the perfect, sustainable product 
for the 21st century and beyond.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:02.334
So, the cork they harvested is put on this truck,
 which goes to the factory.

00:08:02.334 --> 00:08:04.600
And then, in the factory, they’re going to process it into things.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.640
They’re going to granulate it, chop it...

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:10.800
So, we're going to now have a look at the factory,
 to see how it's being processed.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.040
After the harvest, we went to a factory where they process it.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.600
It turns out there are three different ways to process the cork:

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:33.080
Natural, agglomerated and expanded cork.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.080
Here, Charles explains the difference,
 using the classic wine stopper as an example.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:44.840
So, you saw in the forest that we took off 
those big planks of cork off the trees.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.360
Well, that's the first step into making natural wine corks.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:53.080
So, those big planks we saw in
the forest are now cut up into strips.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:57.880
This would be the outside of the tree,
and here would be the inside of the tree.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:03.496
Then, to make a natural wine cork,
which is the most premium kind of wine cork...

00:09:03.496 --> 00:09:04.440
You literally have a punch.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:08.880
And, it punches through the cork,
and pushes the cork out the other side.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.680
At the end of it, you have a natural cork.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:19.640
So, natural corks, the most prized kind of corks, 
and you see a very premium wine cork looks like this.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.640
With almost no imperfections on the surface of it.

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:27.960
So, to get really good quality wine corks,
you need to have really good quality cork.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:31.520
Once you've made the wine corks,
you have all this leftover cork.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:33.000
What happens to this?

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:39.112
It gets granulated turned into granules,
and then those granules can be glued together.

00:09:39.112 --> 00:09:45.122
To make either to make boards like this one, 
which you see for flooring or for notice boards.

00:09:45.122 --> 00:09:48.720
Or, they can be made into agglomerated wine corks.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.848
So, the agglomerated wine cork is much more uniform, than a natural wine cork,

00:09:54.848 --> 00:10:01.880
But, of course, it isn't 100% natural, because the granules have 
to be glued together to make agglomerated cork.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:06.930
What’s left after all of that cork has been used
 for agglomerated cork and natural cork?

00:10:07.529 --> 00:10:10.920
It's rotten bits of cork, which no one really wants...

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:15.480
So, for example, the branches of the trees,
which have been trimmed, like this one.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.541
This is what we've come to see today.

00:10:18.541 --> 00:10:22.414
It's how all this waste cork, which can't be used for anything else...

00:10:22.414 --> 00:10:27.600
How it can be turned into an amazing insulation material,
which is expanded cork.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:36.000
And then, this bark can then be agglomerated,
using a different process to make expanded cork.

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:43.280
One of the key differences about this expanded cork,
is that unlike the agglomerated cork here...

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:46.600
The expanded cork is made in a different way.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:57.600
The resin, which is present in the cork branches itself, 
is used to bind the expanded cork granules together.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.440
So, this is a 100% natural product,
just like natural corks are.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.080
- You would say this thing is cool?
100% natural? Straight from the bark?

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:07.800
Yeah!

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:09.794
- This one less because you need glue?

00:11:09.794 --> 00:11:12.360
It’s cool that it's leftovers, but you need glue to put it together...

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:17.647
This thing is also 100% natural,
because the resin in the cork tree glues it all together.

00:11:17.647 --> 00:11:18.600
- That's exactly right!

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:25.760
What's amazing about this whole process, is that from the bark
 that comes off the tree: not one grain is wasted!

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.840
It can all be used in different ways.

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:31.960
And, in fact, the dust that's produced,
when you're doing this process...

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:37.640
It's then used to make energy for the factories,
to be able to treat the cork itself.

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:40.120
It's a completely circular process.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.080
So, are we going to have a look at the factory?

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:44.119
- Let's go!

00:11:44.903 --> 00:11:45.755
Here, you can see the factory.

00:11:45.755 --> 00:11:47.903
At this one, they make expanded cork.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:54.160
The one that is granulated, but processed 100% natural,
because the resin in the tree is the glue.

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:57.960
The factory looks peaceful from up here,
however, the factory's loud.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:01.000
Shredders, steam, compressed air floating around...

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:04.320
You can't hear anything on camera,
so we got a tour from the boss.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:05.344
A very nice guy!

00:12:05.344 --> 00:12:08.040
I'll narrate the process for you, once we get inside.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:11.320
So, let's start at the beginning of making expanded cork.

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:12.540
The raw material!

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:14.320
They have mountains of cork!

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.560
So, here we are, at an expanded cork factory.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:31.080
Expanded cork is one of the most amazing
insulation materials to ever come across.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:34.520
100% natural and a fantastic insulator!

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.500
And, what's one of the really great things about it is this:

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:42.000
For expanded cork, we use the really rough bits of cork,
 that no one else wants to use.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:46.935
So, this cork I've got in my hand here,
and this massive pile you can see behind me...

00:12:46.935 --> 00:12:50.240
It's scraps of cork that essentially no one else wants to use.

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.760
And here, at the factory, we’re going to see 
how we’re going to take this cork...

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:57.760
Granulate it, and sort it, so we get the best bits that we need.

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:00.155
To make the insulation expanded cork boards

00:13:00.155 --> 00:13:05.960
And then, make it into big blocks, 
which can then be cut into sheets of expanded cork.

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.960
To insulate your home or whatever else needs insulating.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:11.080
So, let's go and visit a factory!

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.880
The first step is to filter out the piles of cork.

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:16.880
Because, all the cork is leftover and mixed with sand and stones...

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:20.760
But, also wood from the cork tree itself
can still be stuck on the bark...

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:25.200
So, they throw it into big machines
that shake it and get the rough dirt out.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:28.080
And then, the shredder,
that chops it into small granules.

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:32.280
They're transported on screws and conveyor belts.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:35.280
All with different systems to filter out unwanted materials!

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:40.520
At this point, it's a mix of different granule sizes of cork, 
so they also separate them.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:44.400
The small ones can be used for expanding,
and will be used for biomass.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.880
The large ones will go back in the system to shred again...

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:50.640
The medium size continues the process.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:53.640
This is what the granules look like
after sorting and shredding.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:57.533
Then, they go into this beast: the boiler.

00:13:57.533 --> 00:14:02.917
Here, they use the biomass leftover from before,
to heat it up and produce steam from water.

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:07.412
He mentioned that the boiler was installed in the seventies, 
and it's still running!

00:14:07.412 --> 00:14:08.960
A very basic and durable machine.

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:13.320
The room down here is fired up
every morning at seven, until midnight.

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.440
It’s 1000 degrees in there!

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:16.200
Yeah, we didn't go in...

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:22.040
Then, it goes to the next step, which is the core
 of the process: the actual pressing.

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:26.200
Here, the core granules go into a big metal mold.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:29.760
In this case, the shape of a big rectangular block.

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:34.320
Once of the mold is full, they close it, heat it up,
and with steam and heat...

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.320
The cork expands and the resin comes out.

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:40.440
Meanwhile, it's pressed together with a big hydraulic press.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:46.240
The process of filling the mold, heating it up,
 and taking it out takes about 20 minutes.

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:50.328
And once it comes out, the block
has a deep brown color because of the heat.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:53.600
The cork itself doesn't burn, but just turns dark.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:57.480
But, inside these blocks, you can actually
see a few little red glowing things.

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.240
These are little pieces of wood,
that didn't manage to filter out.

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:04.320
They did burn and turned into charcoal.

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.160
But, the amount of wood is minimal,
so it's not really a problem.

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:10.320
And, from there, the block is cut open,
so it can cool down faster.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:12.632
With water being sprinkled on top.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.400
At this point, you have a solid block of expanded cork.

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:20.680
Naturally glued together with just heat and pressure.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:23.280
These blocks will rest outside for a few weeks.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:25.233
So they get rid of all the tension inside...

00:15:25.233 --> 00:15:27.960
And then, to the final step: cutting.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:32.000
Here, the blocks are brought in and cut to size.

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:34.840
The blocks feel solid, but are lightweight!

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:39.273
The popular purpose for them is insulation, 
since it has a high 'R'-value.

00:15:39.273 --> 00:15:41.280
But, it also has a few other cool properties!

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:45.440
As for instance, you saw before the wood burnt
and the cork didn’t.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:51.600
So, it's very fire resistant and it's 100% natural material,
which is very unique for an insulator.

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:56.560
Some people use it between walls, roofs,
floors, or even directly on the outside.

00:15:57.320 --> 00:15:59.465
The application defines the thickness you need.

00:15:59.465 --> 00:16:01.440
Thinner for the walls, and thicker for the roofs.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:06.160
So, this is the end result: sheets of expanded cork.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.840
You can have them in different sizes and thicknesses.

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:11.880
We want to use this for our ruin as an insulation material.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:15.360
We need to figure out what's the size we want, 
and what the thickness is.

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:18.440
So, we need to do some calculations and measurements.

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:21.440
But, yeah, we're going to see more
about that later on!

00:16:28.944 --> 00:16:31.480
Alright, so, that was it for this video!

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:35.690
If you already want to see the next one for next week, 
make sure to subscribe on Patreon!

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:38.185
If not, you’ll just see us here next week.

00:16:38.185 --> 00:16:39.823
Same channel, same time!

00:16:39.823 --> 00:16:40.952
Thanks for watching!

